      "text": "To find the \\( 1314^{\\text{th}} \\) digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\), we first need to determine the decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\).\n\n### Step 1: Compute the Decimal Expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\)\nWe start by performing the long division of 5 by 14.\n\n1. **Division Setup:**\n   \\[\n   5 \\div 14 = 0.357142857142\\ldots\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the Decimal Expansion:**\n   The decimal expansion of \\(\\dfrac{5}{14}\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\dfrac{5}{14} = 0.\\overline{357142}\n   \\]\n   This indicates that the decimal repeats every 6 digits: \\(357142\\).\n\n### Step 2: Determine the Position of the 1314th Digit\nWe need to find the position of the \\( 1314^{\\text{th}} \\) digit within the repeating cycle. This can be done by finding the remainder when 1314 is divided by the length of the repeating cycle.\n\n3. **Calculate the Remainder:**\n   \\[\n   1314 \\div 6 = 219 \\text{ remainder } 0\n   \\]\n\n4. **Interpret the Remainder:**\n   A remainder of 0 indicates that the 1314th digit corresponds to the last digit of the repeating block.\n\n### Step 3: Identify the Last Digit of the Repeating Block\nThe repeating block is \\(357142\\). The last digit of this block is \\(2\\).\n\n### Step 4: Conclude the \\( 1314^{\\text{th}} \\) Digit\nSince the 1314th digit corresponds to the last digit of the repeating block, the \\( 1314^{\\text{th}} \\) digit is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
